<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Staffordshire Hoard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">found <a href="/years/2009">2009</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a> Hoard is the largest hoard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> metalwork yet found, discovered by a metal detectorist in a field near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a> in 2009. It contains several thousand pieces, mostly fittings stripped from swords and other war gear of the 7th century, many set with garnets. It is shared between museums in Birmingham and Stoke-on-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/staffordshire-hoard.jpg" alt="Staffordshire Hoard" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Rowan, Birmingham Museum and Art Gallery · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Staffordshire_hoard_annotated.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
